e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265843 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94dec214-fc61-500b-bffe-c52024a2504b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265843 #edb/ref 1074 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-2x}$ and $x{e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4{e}^{-x}$ is not a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345999 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{x/2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345999 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345999 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b85ad48-9f95-542b-b9e7-333573122a0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346157 #edb/ref 1080 "y=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346157 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346157 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5df5ae30-aca2-5b8b-a